{
  "abstract": "Papers 93, 95 and 98 build Diophantine septuples over the function field of an elliptic curve E from a source u, a function on E with u(P)u(P + T) constant for a 2-torsion point T (family I). In degree two, u is a Möbius function of x, and the pinning law of Paper 98 shows that option-(i) edges at two shifts that differ by a 2-torsion point force √−1. We test degree four, with sources that are still functions of x. On the Tate curve with a point g of order 6, option-(i) edges at g and 2g give 17 equations in 13 unknowns. We find an exact point over a number field K of degree 12 with four real places: all 17 equations vanish in K, every degeneracy guard is a nonzero element of K, and u is defined over K. So the √−1 of the pinning law does not come from the pole of u in degree four. It moves to the edge constants. The edge at S is defined over a field L ⊇ K iff a constant m8_S is a square in L, and −m8_g·m8_2g is a square in K. Hence both edges are defined over L only if −1 is a square in L, and at each real place one of the two edges is not real. On K, m8_g·m8_2g equals the toll x(3g) − x(T) of Paper 98 up to squares. Mod-p evidence: exhaustive enumeration at 21 primes with the curve parameter free and at 83 primes on the cubic fibre finds only reductions of conjugates of this point, and on all 1060 nondegenerate degree-two points found, m8_g·m8_2g equals the toll mod squares. The curve is defined over a cubic field, so no rational septuple follows.",
